{"name":"Sentimental Lady","hex":"#c4d3dc","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sentimental","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Characterized by sentiment, sentimentality or excess emotion; appealing to the emotions (especially tender emotions) rather than reason, morality, aesthetic taste, etc.","Derived from emotion rather than logical reason."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sentimental"},{"term":"lady","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The mistress of a household.","A woman of breeding or higher class, a woman of authority."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To address as “lady”."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/lady"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sentimental Lady","blocks":[{"text":"\"Sentimental Lady\" is a song written by Bob Welch. It was originally recorded by Fleetwood Mac on their 1972 album Bare Trees, and was later recorded by Welch on his debut solo album, French Kiss, in 1977. It is a romantic song, originally written for Welch's first wife. Welch recorded it again in 2003 for his album His Fleetwood Mac Years & Beyond."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/7d/Sentimental_Lady_77.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sentimental_Lady"},{"title":"Lady","blocks":[{"text":"A lady is a woman who behaves in a polite way. Once used to describe only women of a high social class or status, the female counterpart of lord, now it may refer to any adult woman, as gentleman can be used for men."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lady"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/John_William_Waterhouse_-_The_Lady_of_Shalott_-_Google_Art_Project_edit.jpg/500px-John_William_Waterhouse_-_The_Lady_of_Shalott_-_Google_Art_Project_edit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/John_William_Waterhouse_-_The_Lady_of_Shalott_-_Google_Art_Project_edit.jpg/1280px-John_William_Waterhouse_-_The_Lady_of_Shalott_-_Google_Art_Project_edit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/John_William_Waterhouse_-_The_Lady_of_Shalott_-_Google_Art_Project_edit.jpg/500px-John_William_Waterhouse_-_The_Lady_of_Shalott_-_Google_Art_Project_edit.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"John William Waterhouse's The Lady of Shalott, 1888 (Tate Gallery, London, England)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJohn_William_Waterhouse_-_The_Lady_of_Shalott_-_Google_Art_Project_edit.jpg"}],"sounds":{"sentimental":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7c/En-us-sentimental.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/7/7c/En-us-sentimental.ogg/En-us-sentimental.ogg.mp3"}],"lady":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/62/En-us-lady.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/6/62/En-us-lady.ogg/En-us-lady.ogg.mp3"}]}}}